---
export const prerender = true;

import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';

// Breadcrumbs run Home > Guides > this page, matching /portable-storage-vs-buying-a-container/.
// One array feeds both the JSON-LD BreadcrumbList (via BaseLayout) and the visible trail.
const crumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
  { name: 'Guides', path: '/shipping-container-guides/' },
  { name: 'Container Certification Guide' },
];

// Single source for the page FAQ. These three strings ARE the FAQPage JSON-LD answers and they
// also render in the QuickFacts card, so each answer stands up quoted alone.
const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'Do I need a CSC plate or certification for a storage container?',
    a: 'No. CSC certification under the 1972 convention applies only to containers in '
      + 'international transport. A container used for storage, or moved by truck or rail inside '
      + 'the United States, needs no CSC plate or examination status. A lapsed status has no '
      + 'effect on storage use.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is a wind and water tight (WWT) container certified for shipping?',
    a: 'No. Wind and water tight is a dealer condition grade meaning the container is '
      + 'structurally sound and keeps out weather. It is not a certification, and it does not '
      + 'make a container eligible for ocean transport. Ocean carriers expect a valid CSC plate '
      + 'and a current inspection certificate from a qualified examiner.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How long does a container recertification survey last?',
    a: 'US regulations at 49 CFR 452.1 cap examination intervals at 30 months. In commercial '
      + 'practice, surveyors and ocean carriers typically treat a survey certificate as current '
      + 'for about six to 18 months at their discretion, so confirm what your carrier expects '
      + 'when you book.',
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  pageType="guide"
  title="Shipping Container Certification: CSC Plates, Surveys & What They Mean | Steel Box Direct"
  description="What a CSC plate is, who actually needs one, and how recertification surveys work. Plain answers for storage buyers and anyone planning to ship overseas."
  datePublished="2026-08-24"
  dateModified="2026-08-24"
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
  schema={{
    kind: 'guide',
    title: 'Shipping Container Certification: CSC Plates, Surveys, and What They Mean',
    specs: [
      { k: 'Certification exists for', v: 'International ocean shipping' },
      { k: 'Needed for storage use', v: 'No' },
      { k: 'Needed for domestic trucking', v: 'No' },
      { k: 'US rules', v: '49 CFR Parts 450 through 453' },
      { k: 'Exam interval ceiling', v: '30 months (49 CFR 452.1)' },
      { k: 'What we sell', v: 'Used WWT, for storage' },
    ],
    faqs,
  }}
>

  <!-- 1. HERO -->
  <div class="cg-hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <Breadcrumbs items={crumbs} />
      <div class="cg-eyebrow">
        <span class="idx">§ CERTIFICATION</span>
        <span class="nm">CSC Plates, Surveys, Examination Status</span>
        <span class="tg">Plain English</span>
      </div>
      <h1 class="cg-headline">Shipping container certification: CSC plates, surveys, and <em>what they mean</em></h1>

      <div class="cg-statement">
        <span class="m cg-statement-k">Read this first</span>
        <p>Are you storing, or are you shipping?</p>
        <p class="cg-statement-body">
          Every shipping container carries a metal plate that once cleared it for the open ocean.
          Most container sites wave at that plate and move on. This guide explains what it
          actually is, when it matters, and the one question that decides everything: are you
          storing, or are you shipping?
        </p>
      </div>

      <ul class="cg-hero-jump">
        <li><a href="#short">The short answer</a></li>
        <li><a href="#plate">The CSC plate</a></li>
        <li><a href="#pes-acep">PES vs ACEP</a></li>
        <li><a href="#grades">The grades ladder</a></li>
        <li><a href="#survey">The survey</a></li>
        <li><a href="#carriers">What carriers demand</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- 2. THE SHORT ANSWER -->
  <section class="cg-short" id="short" aria-labelledby="short-h">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="cg-sh">
        <span class="m">Start Here</span>
        <h2 class="cg-h2" id="short-h">The short answer</h2>
        <p class="cg-sh-sub">
          Certification exists for one purpose: international shipping.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="cg-fork-grid">
        <div class="cg-fork-card is-store">
          <span class="m cg-fork-k">If you are storing</span>
          <h3>No certification of any kind</h3>
          <p>
            A container that sits on your property and stores things needs no certification of any
            kind. A container moving by truck inside the United States needs none either. The
            rules only wake up when a box is lifted onto a ship or crosses a border.
          </p>
          <p class="cg-fork-verdict">If that is not your plan, you can stop reading now and go pick a size.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="cg-fork-card is-ship">
          <span class="m cg-fork-k">If you are shipping</span>
          <h3>Read on</h3>
          <p>
            If putting the box on a ship is your plan, read on, because most of what dealers say
            about "certified" containers is looser than the actual rules.
          </p>
          <p class="cg-fork-verdict">The rest of this page is for you.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 3. THE CSC PLATE + INTERNATIONAL VS DOMESTIC -->
  <section class="cg-plate" id="plate" aria-labelledby="plate-h">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="cg-sh">
        <span class="m">The Plate</span>
        <h2 class="cg-h2" id="plate-h">What the CSC plate is, and who requires it</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="cg-prose">
        <p>
          Open the left door of almost any container and you will find a riveted metal plate
          marked "CSC Safety Approval." That plate exists because of an international treaty: the
          International Convention for Safe Containers, adopted through the IMO in 1972, usually
          shortened to CSC 1972. The treaty's goal is simple. Containers get stacked nine high on
          ships and lifted by their corners thousands of times. The plate certifies that the
          design can take those loads.
        </p>
        <p>
          In the United States, the treaty is implemented through federal regulations at 49 CFR
          Parts 450 through 453, administered by the US Coast Guard. Those rules govern who
          approves container designs, how containers must be examined over their working life,
          and what the plate must show.
        </p>
        <p class="cg-callout">
          Here is the part that matters for buyers: the CSC regime applies to containers in
          international transport. That is the treaty's whole scope.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="cg-sub-hd">
        <h3>International vs domestic: the distinction that decides everything</h3>
        <p>This is the wall the entire topic hangs on, so it gets its own section.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="cg-prose">
        <p>
          A container needs a valid CSC examination status to be loaded on a ship for
          international carriage. It does not need one to sit in a farmyard in Ohio. It does not
          need one to ride a tilt-bed truck from a depot to your property. It does not need one
          to move by road or rail inside the country. Domestic transport and storage sit outside
          the CSC system entirely, a point the Bureau International des Containers makes in its
          own explainer on the convention.
        </p>
        <p>
          So a used container whose examination status lapsed years ago is not "illegal" or
          "expired" in any way that affects a storage buyer. It is simply no longer cleared for
          the one job it retired from: crossing oceans. For everything a storage buyer does with
          it, the plate is history, not paperwork.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 4. PES VS ACEP + LEAVING THE FLEET -->
  <section class="cg-pes" id="pes-acep" aria-labelledby="pes-h">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="cg-sh">
        <span class="m">The System</span>
        <h2 class="cg-h2" id="pes-h">PES vs ACEP, in plain words</h2>
        <p class="cg-sh-sub">
          The CSC system gives container owners two ways to keep a box in good standing.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="cg-two-grid">
        <div class="cg-card is-pes">
          <span class="m cg-card-k">Scheme one</span>
          <h3>PES, the Periodic Examination Scheme</h3>
          <p>
            Works like a car inspection. The container gets its first examination within five
            years of manufacture, then re-examinations at intervals of no more than 30 months,
            under 49 CFR 452.1. Each passing exam earns a decal showing the Next Examination
            Date, usually shortened to NED. If the date on the decal is in the future, the box is
            current. If it has passed, the status has lapsed.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="cg-card is-acep">
          <span class="m cg-card-k">Scheme two</span>
          <h3>ACEP, the Approved Continuous Examination Program</h3>
          <p>
            How the big fleets do it. Instead of dated stickers on millions of boxes, a shipping
            line or leasing company runs a Coast-Guard-approved inspection program covering its
            whole fleet, under 49 CFR 452.7. Boxes under ACEP carry a marking in the form
            ACEP/USA followed by a year, and no NED decal at all. The box is covered as long as
            it belongs to that owner's program.
          </p>
          <p class="cg-trapdoor">That last clause is the trapdoor, and it leads to the next section.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="cg-sub-hd">
        <h3>What happens when a container leaves the fleet</h3>
      </div>

      <div class="cg-prose">
        <p>
          Here is the subtlety that trips up almost everyone, including plenty of dealers: the
          CSC plate does not expire when a container is sold. The plate certifies the design, and
          it stays riveted to the door for the life of the box. What changes at sale is the
          examination status.
        </p>
        <p>
          When a shipping line retires a container and sells it, the line removes the box from
          its ACEP. The program that covered it no longer applies, and there is usually no valid
          NED decal underneath, because fleet boxes never needed one. The Container Owners
          Association spells out the consequence in its technical guidance, COA TG03: once the
          box is sold out of the fleet, the buyer becomes responsible for its CSC maintenance.
        </p>
        <p>
          Age tells you which side of the line a box sits on. A one-trip container is typically
          still inside its first five-year window from manufacture. The used boxes that fill
          dealer yards were generally retired after a decade or more of service, long past any
          exam that covered them. Either way, if you ever want the box on a ship again, restoring
          its examination status becomes your job, not the seller's.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 5. THE GRADES LADDER -->
  <section class="cg-grades" id="grades" aria-labelledby="grades-h">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="cg-sh cg-sh-dark">
        <span class="m">Condition Grades</span>
        <h2 class="cg-h2" id="grades-h">The grades ladder: WWT, CW, IICL-6, and why none of them is a certification</h2>
      </div>

      <p class="cg-table-caption" id="cg-grades-cap">
        Dealers grade used containers, and the grades form a rough ladder.
        <span class="cg-table-hint">Scroll the table sideways on a small screen</span>
      </p>
      <div class="cg-tbl-scroll" role="region" aria-labelledby="cg-grades-cap" tabindex="0">
        <table class="cg-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col" class="cg-rowhead"><span class="m">Grade</span></th>
              <th scope="col"><span class="cg-th-title">What it means</span></th>
              <th scope="col"><span class="cg-th-title">Is it a certification?</span></th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Wind and water tight (WWT)</th>
              <td>A dealer grade, not a shipping standard. It means the box is structurally sound and keeps weather out. It is the workhorse grade for storage. This is the grade Steel Box Direct sells, and it is sold for storage, not for shipping.</td>
              <td>No</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Cargo worthy (CW)</th>
              <td>The box is judged fit to carry cargo at sea. Useful, but be careful: the criteria vary from seller to seller unless the grade is tied to a named standard, such as the COA's Common Cargo Worthy criteria or the UCIRC repair criteria.</td>
              <td>No</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">IICL-6</th>
              <td>The strictest common standard, used by leasing companies, with tighter tolerances than cargo worthy.</td>
              <td>No</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="cg-flip">
        <span class="m cg-flip-k">The sentence that saves people money and grief</span>
        <h3>A grade describes physical condition. Certification is a regulatory status with a date on it.</h3>
        <p>
          None of these grades is a CSC examination status. Buying a container advertised as
          cargo worthy does not mean it has a valid exam. Carriers know the difference even when
          listings blur it.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 6. THE SURVEY -->
  <section class="cg-survey" id="survey" aria-labelledby="survey-h">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="cg-sh">
        <span class="m">Recertification</span>
        <h2 class="cg-h2" id="survey-h">What a recertification survey covers, and who can perform one</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="cg-prose">
        <p>
          To put a lapsed box back in good standing, a qualified examiner inspects it against
          industry criteria and, if it passes, affixes a new NED decal and issues a report. The
          survey works over the whole structure: corner castings and corner posts, top and bottom
          rails, cross members and the underside (which is why the box usually goes up on a
          forklift), the floor, the doors and their gaskets, the wall panels, the roof bows, and
          overall weathertightness.
        </p>
        <p>
          Who is allowed to do this? Less than you would guess. 49 CFR 452.3 requires only that
          examinations be performed by "qualified personnel, trained and experienced." There is
          no US government license called container surveyor, and "licensed maritime inspector"
          on a vendor page is loose language. In practice, the credential that carries weight is
          certification from the IICL, the Institute of International Container Lessors, earned
          through a proctored exam and renewed on a five-year cycle. The IICL publishes the only
          real directory of certified inspectors, and container depots coordinate most surveys,
          since the box, the forklift, and the inspector are all in one place.
        </p>
        <p>
          One exception worth knowing: containers that have been structurally modified are a
          different animal. Under 49 CFR 451, modified designs need review by an
          administration-appointed Approval Authority, such as ABS acting under Coast Guard
          delegation. A standard survey cannot bless a cut-up box.
        </p>
        <p>
          On cost, published figures from providers give the range. IPL publishes survey pricing
          of $75 to $250 per container. A greater-Cincinnati inspector publishes a rate of $125
          per hour including travel, plus a depot handling fee. Your number depends on where the
          box is and who examines it, but that is the published landscape.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="cg-sub-hd">
        <h3>How long a survey is good for</h3>
        <p>Two clocks run at once here, and they are different clocks.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="cg-clock-grid">
        <div class="cg-clock">
          <span class="m cg-clock-k">The regulatory clock</span>
          <p>
            The regulatory ceiling is 30 months: under the PES rules, no examination interval may
            exceed it.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="cg-clock">
          <span class="m cg-clock-k">The commercial clock</span>
          <p>
            Commercial practice is tighter. Surveyors and carriers commonly treat a survey
            certificate as current for something like six to 18 months, at their discretion, so
            confirm the window with your carrier when you book. A carrier booking cargo wants
            recent paper rather than a decal with two years left on it.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="cg-clock-note">
        Meeting the regulation keeps you legal. Meeting the carrier's expectation gets you
        booked. You need both, and they are not the same thing.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 7. WHAT CARRIERS DEMAND -->
  <section class="cg-carriers" id="carriers" aria-labelledby="carriers-h">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="cg-sh cg-sh-dark">
        <span class="m">The Finish Line</span>
        <h2 class="cg-h2" id="carriers-h">What carriers actually demand</h2>
      </div>

      <p class="cg-table-caption" id="cg-carrier-cap">
        The carrier is the final gatekeeper, so its checklist is the real finish line.
        Hapag-Lloyd publishes its requirements for shipper-owned containers, and they are
        concrete. Other carriers publish their own versions.
        <span class="cg-table-hint">Scroll the table sideways on a small screen</span>
      </p>
      <div class="cg-tbl-scroll" role="region" aria-labelledby="cg-carrier-cap" tabindex="0">
        <table class="cg-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col" class="cg-rowhead"><span class="m">Item</span></th>
              <th scope="col"><span class="cg-th-title">Hapag-Lloyd's published requirement</span></th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Plate and paper</th>
              <td>A valid CSC plate and a current inspection certificate</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Stacking weight allowance</th>
              <td>At least 192,000 kg</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Racking test value</th>
              <td>At least 15,000 kg</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Dimensions</th>
              <td>Conforming to ISO 668</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Identification</th>
              <td>A proper ISO 6346 identification number</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p class="cg-carrier-note">
        The pattern is the same: the plate, current paper, and a box that meets the structural
        minimums. No certificate, no booking.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 8. THE HONEST BOTTOM LINE -->
  <section class="cg-bottom" aria-labelledby="bottom-h">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="cg-sh">
        <span class="m">The Honest Bottom Line</span>
        <h2 class="cg-h2" id="bottom-h">Enjoy knowing about it, or work backward from the booking</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="cg-two-grid">
        <div class="cg-card is-store">
          <span class="m cg-card-k">Buying for storage</span>
          <h3>Do not pay extra for it</h3>
          <p>
            If you are buying a container for storage, a workshop, or any use that keeps it on
            the ground, certification is a topic you can enjoy knowing about and never act on.
            Nothing above applies to you. Do not pay extra for it.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="cg-card is-ship">
          <span class="m cg-card-k">Shipping internationally</span>
          <h3>Work backward from the booking</h3>
          <p>
            If you know you will ship internationally, work backward from the booking. Buy a
            cargo worthy container from a depot, tell the seller it is for export, and arrange
            the survey when you book, because your carrier will want a fresh certificate anyway.
            What almost never pays is buying a storage-grade box and trying to certify it up
            later. That path runs through a survey, possible structural repairs, and your
            carrier's judgment, and it usually costs more than starting with the right box.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 9. WHERE STEEL BOX DIRECT FITS -->
  <section class="cg-sbd" aria-labelledby="sbd-h">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="cg-sh cg-sh-dark">
        <span class="m">Where We Fit</span>
        <h2 class="cg-h2" id="sbd-h">Where Steel Box Direct fits</h2>
      </div>

      <p class="cg-sbd-note">
        Steel Box Direct sells one honest grade: used wind and water tight containers, for
        storage and on-site use, not certified for ocean shipping. That is stated plainly on our
        <a href="/condition/">condition guide</a>, which explains exactly what WWT means in
        practice. If a WWT box fits your plan, our
        <a href="/shipping-containers-for-sale/">containers for sale</a> page covers the three
        sizes we stock. And if you want to decode the plate and markings on a box you already
        own, the <a href="/container-reference/">container reference</a> walks through ISO 6346
        IDs, the CSC plate, and full dimensions in plain English.
      </p>
      <div class="cg-cta-btns">
        <a href="/quote/" class="btn cg-btn-yellow">Get My Quote →</a>
        <a href="/condition/" class="btn-ghost">What WWT means</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="cg-next" aria-labelledby="next-h">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="cg-next-label" id="next-h">Continue researching</p>
      <div class="cg-next-links">
        <a href="/condition/">What does "used" mean?</a>
        <a href="/container-reference/">Decode the markings</a>
        <a href="/shipping-containers-for-sale/">Containers for sale</a>
        <a href="/shipping-container-guides/">All container guides</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

</BaseLayout>

<style>
  /* ---- SHARED SECTION HEADER (pattern from /portable-storage-vs-buying-a-container/) ---- */
  .cg-sh{border-top:4px solid var(--ink);padding-top:18px;margin-bottom:40px}
  .cg-sh .m{display:block;opacity:.55;margin-bottom:10px}
  .cg-sh-dark{border-top-color:var(--yellow)}
  .cg-sh-dark .m{color:var(--yellow);opacity:1}
  .cg-h2{font-family:var(--narrow);font-weight:700;text-transform:uppercase;font-size:clamp(28px,4.2vw,46px);line-height:1;letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0}
  .cg-sh-sub{font-size:17px;line-height:1.55;max-width:820px;margin:18px 0 0;opacity:.82}
  .cg-sub-hd{margin:52px 0 24px;border-top:2px solid rgba(11,15,26,.15);padding-top:22px}
